From: ViKing <viking.yfy@qq.com>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 08:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] netfilter: flowtable: carry a priority into the offload

Packets forwarded by the flowtable skip the ruleset, so a priority set
by "meta priority set" before "flow add" only applies to the first
packets of a connection, and drivers never see it.

Store the priority of the packet that creates the flow, apply it in the
software fast path and emit it as FLOW_ACTION_PRIORITY, the action
act_skbedit uses. The priority applies to both directions.

On IPv4, ip_forward() also gives every packet a priority derived from its
TOS while ip_forward_update_priority is on, the default. That priority
belongs to each packet, not to the flow: storing the first packet's would
fix it for the connection, and only on IPv4, since IPv6 forwarding sets
none. So a priority that equals the TOS-derived one is not stored, and
for such flows the fast path derives it per packet as ip_forward() does.
Hardware is given no priority for them and classifies on the DSCP.

airoha and mtk ignore it like FLOW_ACTION_CSUM (on 6.18 airoha takes the
QoS queue of a flow from its DSCP), and mlx5 ignores it on flowtable
flows (previous patch). airoha and mtk parse tc flower rules in the same
function, so they now also accept skbedit priority there.

Based-on-patch-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
Signed-off-by: ViKing <viking.yfy@qq.com>
---
--- a/Documentation/networking/nf_flowtable.rst
+++ b/Documentation/networking/nf_flowtable.rst
@@ -71,7 +71,10 @@ forwarding path including the Netfilter
 
 The flowtable entry also stores the NAT configuration, so all packets are
 mangled according to the NAT policy that is specified from the classic IP
-forwarding path. The TTL is decremented before calling neigh_xmit(). Fragmented
+forwarding path. The TTL is decremented before calling neigh_xmit(). The flow
+also stores a priority set before ``flow add``, so it applies to the packets that
+the flowtable forwards; an IPv4 priority derived from the TOS, as with
+ip_forward_update_priority, is derived per packet as in the classic path. Fragmented
 traffic is passed up to follow the classic IP forwarding path given that the
 transport header is missing, in this case, flowtable lookups are not possible.
 TCP RST and FIN packets are also passed up to the classic IP forwarding path to
